1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,640
Tuesday, twenty to nine in the evening.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:07,440
A buyer pays for roses on Malika's shop, and the payment page just spins.

3
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:10,728
A minute later, Malika is on the phone to the bank.

4
00:00:10,778 --> 00:00:14,232
Dilnoza, in the bank's operations team, picks up.

5
00:00:14,282 --> 00:00:18,624
Malika says her customer's payment has been spinning for a whole minute.

6
00:00:18,674 --> 00:00:25,464
That payment crossed the payment page, ecom, the card zone, an adapter and the processing centre.

7
00:00:25,514 --> 00:00:29,352
Four machines touched it, and each one writes its own log lines.

8
00:00:29,402 --> 00:00:33,264
Without one number to follow, Dilnoza would search four logs and guess.

9
00:00:33,314 --> 00:00:37,464
And the lines that would help her most are the ones most likely to hold a card number.

10
00:00:37,514 --> 00:00:46,608
In Lesson 24, this path was built end to end, and in Lesson 3, card numbers on screens were cut to the first six and the last four digits.

11
00:00:46,658 --> 00:00:52,608
This lesson follows one payment across that whole path, and keeps every log line just as clean.

12
00:00:52,658 --> 00:00:58,872
Every request gets a trace: one number that follows it from the moment it arrives until its answer leaves.

13
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:02,256
When ecom calls the card zone, the trace goes along.

14
00:01:02,306 --> 00:01:05,568
When the card zone calls an adapter, it goes along again.

15
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:09,336
So one request draws one line across both products.

16
00:01:09,386 --> 00:01:13,656
Every answer names its trace too, even an answer that says no.

17
00:01:13,706 --> 00:01:20,280
Bekzod, who connected Malika's site, keeps that number with every order, so Malika reads it out over the phone.

18
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:23,088
Support finds the whole story in seconds.

19
00:01:23,138 --> 00:01:25,704
Every log line carries the same number.

20
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:32,376
So a warning in one log leads straight to the request that caused it, and to every other line about that request.

21
00:01:32,426 --> 00:01:34,248
Now the card numbers.

22
00:01:34,298 --> 00:01:38,184
I think the log is the leakiest place in any payment system.

23
00:01:38,234 --> 00:01:43,584
Most teams lock the database, and then print a card number into a log while chasing a bug.

24
00:01:43,634 --> 00:01:47,256
So here, before any line is written, it is cleaned.

25
00:01:47,306 --> 00:01:49,128
This is log masking.

26
00:01:49,178 --> 00:01:54,864
A card number keeps only its first six and its last four digits; the middle becomes stars.

27
00:01:54,914 --> 00:01:59,184
Any long run of digits that could be a card number is treated as one.

28
00:01:59,234 --> 00:02:02,328
A token keeps only its last four characters.

29
00:02:02,378 --> 00:02:05,304
Passwords and secrets are hidden completely.

30
00:02:05,354 --> 00:02:08,784
This happens on every line, wherever the line is sent.

31
00:02:08,834 --> 00:02:11,928
So nothing the running system writes can leak a card.

32
00:02:11,978 --> 00:02:15,096
Each product sends all of this to its own monitoring.

33
00:02:15,146 --> 00:02:19,128
ecom has its own, and the card zone runs its own, separately.

34
00:02:19,178 --> 00:02:21,816
They share nothing here either, just as on the map.

35
00:02:21,866 --> 00:02:24,648
Dilnoza opens ecom's monitoring.

36
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:30,024
The first dashboard shows payments by how they end: paid, declined, and unknown.

37
00:02:30,074 --> 00:02:35,232
Unknown is painted red: payments where nobody yet knows whether money moved.

38
00:02:35,282 --> 00:02:38,232
Right now, the red line sits at zero.

39
00:02:38,282 --> 00:02:40,752
She pastes the trace number Malika read out.

40
00:02:40,802 --> 00:02:47,304
One line appears, crossing ecom, the card zone and the adapter, with the time each step took.

41
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:51,000
The adapter waited almost a second for the processing centre.

42
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:53,592
She opens the log lines of that request.

43
00:02:53,642 --> 00:02:58,704
The card number shows its first six digits, a row of stars, and its last four.

44
00:02:58,754 --> 00:03:01,800
Later that night, the processing centre goes quiet.

45
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:05,568
Payments start to stay unknown, and the red line climbs.

46
00:03:05,618 --> 00:03:09,480
Dilnoza has gone home, and nobody is watching the screen.

47
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:12,096
That does not matter, because of alert rules.

48
00:03:12,146 --> 00:03:18,936
An alert rule is a condition the system checks on its own, all the time, written down like the rest of the product.

49
00:03:18,986 --> 00:03:23,112
When payments stay unknown for too long, the rule fires.

50
00:03:23,162 --> 00:03:26,712
An email reaches Dilnoza, saying that payments are stuck.

51
00:03:26,762 --> 00:03:29,040
It says what is wrong and when it started.

52
00:03:29,090 --> 00:03:32,976
She opens the dashboard from the email and sees the same red line.

53
00:03:33,026 --> 00:03:37,488
Without the trace, Malika's simple question costs an hour of guessing.

54
00:03:37,538 --> 00:03:41,784
Without masking, the logs become a second place where card numbers live.

55
00:03:41,834 --> 00:03:46,392
Without alerts, a stuck payment is found by an angry buyer, not by the bank.

56
00:03:46,442 --> 00:03:48,408
Tonight, the bank knew first.

57
00:03:48,458 --> 00:03:50,928
Next: what the merchant gets each day.
